    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d344">

                <pb facs="0619" n="503" sameAs="frus:frus1923v01/pg503"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.002/113: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Schurman</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1923-01-25T11:00:00+08:00"><hi rendition="#italic">January 25,
                                1923—11 a.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received January 25—9:10
                        a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>31. Senate yesterday confirmed Cabinet members excepting Sze who failed with 99
                    votes out of 204.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d344-f1"/> Sze was third lowest in House
                    vote January 18. I have reliable information that the reason for Sze’s defeat
                    was that in Cabinet meetings he opposed the re-arrest of Lo Wen-kan<anchor n="3"
                        xml:id="d344-f2"/> after a judicial tribunal had acquitted him.</p>
                <p>The present Cabinet is under the control of militarists and politicians who
                    overturned the Cabinet of Wang Chung-hui<anchor n="4" xml:id="d344-f3"/> by
                    arbitrarily arresting Lo Wen-kan with the authority of the President. <pb
                        facs="0620" n="504" xml:id="pg504"/> This action was instigated by the
                    Speaker of the House. Sze did not appreciate the political situation or the
                    nature of the forces with which he was allied.</p>
                <p>My telegram no. 8 of January 5, noon. Cheng Keh has taken the post of Minister of
                    Justice, C. T. Wang having declined to serve.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Schurman</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d344-f1" n="2" xml:id="d344-f1-content">
                <p>General Huang Fu replaced Sze as Minister for Foreign Affairs on Feb. 9.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d344-f2" n="3" xml:id="d344-f2-content">
                <p>Minister of Finance in Cabinet of Wang Chung-hui.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d344-f3" n="4" xml:id="d344-f3-content">
                <p>In Nov. 1922.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
